face-image: img/tipp.jpg

= Workers' Party of Turkey

A spectre used to haunt Anatolia just like it did across Europe a few decades prior, before it was exorcised by the army in the 12 March Memorandum. Workers' Party of Turkey, TİP, was formed in 1961 after the new liberal constitution lessened political restrictions and allowed for new forms of political expression to arise. The party, even though it was originally founded by non ideological cadres, evolved to represent a vision for Socialism uniquely suited to the circumstances surrounding Turkey and wholly committed to liberal democracy.

The party in spite of hostility across much of the political spectrum managed to score a victory in the 1965 elections and send 15 delegates to the TBMM out of the 450 total representatives. The party, despite its total committence to parliamentary democracy, shook the Turkish elites and bourgeoisie to their core and represented a new vision of progress and welfare for the downtrodden masses.

In terms of ideology, TİP was marked by close ties with the rising syndicates and represented a form of <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Democratic Socialism<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demsoc.jpg">Democratic Socialism is a left-wing ideology that wishes to overthrow Capitalism and establish a Socialist economy through democratic means.</span></span> determined to break out of the political fringes. Differently from much of the other Socialist parties across Europe and the Middle East, the party regularly breached classical Socialist iconography. The party utilized visual instruments in colors such as blue and green and made use of symbols derived from Turkish culture in order to seek appeal in a population that was actively being indoctrinated to fear the stereotypicalized left.

TİP separated into two approaches when it came to party structure: the first was the <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Democratic Socialists<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/demsoc.jpg">Democratic Socialism is a left-wing ideology that wishes to overthrow Capitalism and establish a Socialist economy through democratic means.</span></span>, which had Mehmet Ali Aybar "the Smiling Socialist" as their leader, advocated for a mass party. The second group was the <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Marxist Leninists<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/lenin.jpg">Marxism-Leninism is a far left ideology that wishes to overthrow Capitalism through a revolution which would be led by a vanguard party of ideologically loyal members. Such a party would lead the way towards a world revolution after which Communism, a stateless, moneyless and classless society would be achieved as the final step in human history.</span></span>, headed by a diumvirate of Behice Boran and Sadun Aren, advocated for a vanguard party that would lead the proletariat to socialism. Although Aybar and the Aren-Boran group had initially cooperated in building TİP, relations deteriorated over Aybar’s leadership and the limits of the party’s electoral-parliamentary strategy between 1965 and 1969. After TİP’s heavy defeat in the 1969 election, Aybar resigned as chairman, and the Aren-Boran faction took control of the party in 1970.

Currently TİP is banned after the memorandum on 12 March 1971 and many of its members are imprisoned and undergoing reportedly horrible treatment. Even though the spectre haunting Anatolia has been exorcised, many on the left dream of one day reestablishing the mass party and having it lead a socialist united front committed to real change through democratic means. However Behice Boran holds the rights for party chairmanship due to her status as the leader prior to the ban, and many Socialists do not find resurrecting the fading ghost desirable if it means having the party controlled by the Leninists.

